<attribute name="mainDescription" nodeId="82adbbff">&lt;p&gt;
    Test scripts and test cases are often interchangeably used terms. The meaning of these two terms usually varies
    depending on the experience and perspective of individuals involved in the test effort. Differences in
    meaning are often revealed in the context of manual and automated testing. Generally speaking, test cases specify
    in detail how to test certain aspects of functionality, while test scripts are the actual test implementation,
    i.e., the step-by-step instructions to perform the test. For automated testing, the inputs, execution conditions,
    expected results and other information defined in a test case specify how functionality is validated. The test
    script, which is usually written in a computer programming language, contains the actual instructions to perform the
    test. In manual testing, the difference between test cases and test scripts is often blurred because the instructions
    to perform the validation may be embedded in the specification to validate functionality.
&lt;/p&gt;</attribute>
